<script>
// GET /releases — a feed, newest first, pre-releases marked rather than hidden.
//
// There are no releases. There are no git tags. Writing a release entry here
// would be the one thing this site refuses to do, so the page says what is true
// and shows what the project does have instead: the design record, one cell per
// RFC, counted off the file names while this page was built.
//
// `release.yml` is in the repository and inert until someone pushes a `v*` tag.
// `fresh.js` asks the GitHub feed once per visit, so the first release fills the
// notice at the top of this page without a rebuild.
import { Head } from "std/ui"
import { pageHead } from "../nav"
import { rfcCells, rfcStripWidth, rfcStripHeight, rfcTag, Cell } from "../chart"
import { rfcCount, rfcRows, RfcRow } from "../repo"

export fn head() -> Head {
    return pageHead("Releases — Vyrn")
}

/// The five most recent design records, newest first — the closest thing to a
/// changelog the project has today.
fn latestRfcs() -> Array<RfcRow> {
    let rows = rfcRows()
    let mut out: Array<RfcRow> = []
    let mut i = rows.length - 1
    while i >= 0 && out.length < 8 {
        out.push(RfcRow { number: rows[i].number, title: rows[i].title.copy() })
        i = i - 1
    }
    return out
}
</script>

<template>
<main class="page">
    <aside class="rail">
        <a href="#status"><span class="n">01</span>status</a>
        <a href="#record"><span class="n">02</span>the record</a>
        <a href="#latest"><span class="n">03</span>latest</a>
        <a href="#how"><span class="n">04</span>how it works</a>
    </aside>
        <section class="hero" id="status">
            <p class="eyebrow">Releases</p>
            <h1 class="display">Nothing is released yet</h1>
            <p class="lede">There is no versioned release and no published binary. The language changes without a deprecation period. <a href="/install.html">Build from source</a> — it is one clone and one command.</p>
            <p class="notice" data-release-note hidden="hidden"></p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="record">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 — what there is instead</p>
            <h2>{{ rfcCount() }} design records</h2>
            <p>Every decision in this language was argued in writing before it was built. That record is the closest thing to a changelog the project has, and it is more useful than one: each entry carries the argument, not only the outcome.</p>
            <div class="plate" data-widget="strip">
                <div class="plate-head">
                    <span class="pill">Record</span>
                    <span class="eyebrow">rfcs/</span>
                </div>
                <span class="bignum"><span data-strip-count>{{ rfcCount() }}</span><small>RFCs, RFC-0001 onward</small></span>
                <div class="stage">
                    <svg class="chart" :viewBox='"0 0 " + rfcStripWidth() + " " + rfcStripHeight()' role="img" aria-label="One cell per design record, in order">
                        <rect class="cell" v-for="c in rfcCells()" :key="c.id" :x="c.x" :y="c.y" width="18" height="18" :data-tone="c.tone" fill="currentColor" :fill-opacity="c.tone">
                            <title>{{ c.title }}</title>
                        </rect>
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <p class="cap">One cell per file in rfcs/, counted while this page was built. Hover a cell for its number and title. The tone steps every ten records so the sequence is countable.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="latest">
            <p class="eyebrow">03 — latest</p>
            <h2>The eight most recent</h2>
            <ul class="feed">
                <li v-for="r in latestRfcs()" :key="r.number">
                    <span class="tag">{{ rfcTag(r.number) }}</span>
                    <span>{{ r.title }}</span>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="notice">The whole index, with the status each record carries, is <a href="https://github.com/vyrn-lang/vyrn/blob/main/rfcs/README.md">in the repository</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="band" id="how">
            <p class="eyebrow">04 — how a release will work</p>
            <h2>One tag, three binaries</h2>
            <p>Pushing a <code>v*</code> tag builds the <code>vyrn</code> binary for Linux, macOS and Windows and publishes a GitHub release. A tag with a suffix — <code>v0.2.0-rc.1</code> — publishes as a pre-release, and this page will list it and mark it rather than hide it.</p>
            <p>Until a tag exists the workflow never runs: it has no schedule and no manual trigger. Nothing on this page is waiting on a decision except the decision to cut a version.</p>
            <p class="notice">What is stable today is the verification, not the surface. Every example runs under all three backends and must agree byte for byte, and CI enforces that on every push.</p>
        </section>
</main>
</template>
